WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:06.711
Allow me to introduce myself.

00:00:07.003 --> 00:00:10.006
I’m a writer, journalist

00:00:10.048 --> 00:00:11.299
and a socialist.

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:13.635
I also used to be broke.

00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:15.762
I did, for a long time.

00:00:15.762 --> 00:00:17.055
Being a student,

00:00:17.097 --> 00:00:20.642
trying to make a name for myself
in the media industry

00:00:20.642 --> 00:00:22.352
and starting a media organization.

00:00:22.352 --> 00:00:26.231
I had to move 15 times in 15 years

00:00:26.273 --> 00:00:29.317
because the rent in London
just kept on going up.

00:00:30.402 --> 00:00:35.240
Now, this taught me
an important lesson, firsthand.

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:39.869
Namely, you can want to study
public policy or astrophysics,

00:00:39.911 --> 00:00:42.122
you can want to make a name for yourself,

00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:44.708
you can want to do an honest day’s work.

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:47.460
But all of that
is extraordinarily difficult

00:00:47.502 --> 00:00:50.589
if your mind is thinking
about those unpaid bills.

00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:54.050
Or knowing, with the fullness
of your heart,

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:55.719
that when you go to the cashpoint,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.138
it's going to respond
"Insufficient funds."

00:00:58.847 --> 00:01:01.224
Now, while I was going through all this --

00:01:01.266 --> 00:01:04.769
which, I have to say, happened
a little bit too long for my liking --

00:01:04.811 --> 00:01:09.482
I agreed with the ideas that I was reading
from the liberal tradition,

00:01:09.524 --> 00:01:13.153
namely that individuals
are uniquely placed

00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:16.781
to determine how their lives
should unfold -- nobody else is.

00:01:16.781 --> 00:01:18.408
For everybody in this room,

00:01:18.408 --> 00:01:22.245
the best person to decide
how your life should unfold is you.

00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:25.707
There’s no higher authority,
certainly not the state.

00:01:26.625 --> 00:01:28.251
But what was equally clear

00:01:28.251 --> 00:01:31.504
was that, in the absence of access
to certain resources --

00:01:31.546 --> 00:01:35.133
education, transport,
health care and housing --

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.429
that capacity for self-authorship
is clearly limited for many people.

00:01:39.471 --> 00:01:41.389
We clearly live in a society

00:01:41.431 --> 00:01:44.309
where what we view as liberty,
the pursuit of happiness,

00:01:44.351 --> 00:01:48.355
is limited for the majority
of the global population.

00:01:48.355 --> 00:01:50.440
For many, it's illusory.

00:01:51.858 --> 00:01:55.362
It turns out that liberal ends
of self-authorship,

00:01:55.403 --> 00:01:57.781
of determining
how your life should unfold,

00:01:57.822 --> 00:01:59.616
require socialist means.

00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:02.160
The state must get involved.

00:02:02.661 --> 00:02:06.790
Now, we live in a world where capitalism
has completely prevailed.

00:02:07.207 --> 00:02:08.375
It's won.

00:02:08.416 --> 00:02:10.794
I was asked, behind stage,
"What are you wearing?"

00:02:10.835 --> 00:02:13.046
I said, "Chanel Allure."
And I'm the communist.

00:02:13.088 --> 00:02:14.089
(Laughter)

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.675
OK? So I'm under no illusions --

00:02:16.675 --> 00:02:18.218
it has completely prevailed.

00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:21.429
And the ideas driving it
are those of economic liberalism.

00:02:21.763 --> 00:02:24.766
Economic liberalism
says what I've just said to you.

00:02:24.808 --> 00:02:28.478
The individual is uniquely placed
to determine how their life unfolds,

00:02:28.478 --> 00:02:30.855
and that this should happen
through the market.

00:02:30.897 --> 00:02:32.148
But there's a problem.

00:02:33.024 --> 00:02:35.568
While the market
can be an extraordinary resource

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.905
to get the things you need to get
to be who you want to be,

00:02:38.947 --> 00:02:40.949
for many, it's the opposite.

00:02:40.949 --> 00:02:42.325
It’s a source of un-freedom.

00:02:42.325 --> 00:02:43.702
It's a constraint.

00:02:43.743 --> 00:02:46.204
It's more akin to a system of rationing

00:02:46.246 --> 00:02:48.456
than of prosperity.

00:02:51.251 --> 00:02:55.130
We know this is the case
for an increasing number of people,

00:02:55.171 --> 00:02:57.257
because the numbers don't lie.

00:02:57.257 --> 00:03:02.679
In 2018, 40 million Americans
used food stamps --

00:03:02.721 --> 00:03:06.599
13 million more than in 2007,
before the global financial crisis.

00:03:06.975 --> 00:03:08.560
Clearly, something is broken.

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:10.854
You can disagree with me
about everything else,

00:03:10.854 --> 00:03:12.814
but those are the facts.

00:03:12.856 --> 00:03:15.275
In the UK, a much smaller country,

00:03:16.151 --> 00:03:22.073
in 2016, 17 million people of working age

00:03:22.115 --> 00:03:24.200
had 100 pounds or less savings.

00:03:25.201 --> 00:03:28.163
They are one minor accident
away from penury.

00:03:28.163 --> 00:03:32.041
17 million people of working age.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.171
And on the thing that capitalism
asks to be judged by,

00:03:36.171 --> 00:03:37.922
global growth,

00:03:37.964 --> 00:03:44.929
it says, "All this terrible stuff happens,
inequality, poverty, whatever --

00:03:44.929 --> 00:03:46.723
but we've still got growth."

00:03:46.765 --> 00:03:49.392
Well, actually, the story
isn't so great there either.

00:03:50.185 --> 00:03:53.313
Despite the rise of China,
global growth is in secular decline.

00:03:53.354 --> 00:03:55.982
So, to be clear, the global economy
is still growing,

00:03:55.982 --> 00:03:59.611
but at a smaller, slower rate,
decade on decade.

00:03:59.652 --> 00:04:03.698
20 years ago, if you said
the term "lost decade,"

00:04:03.698 --> 00:04:05.241
you were talking about Japan.

00:04:05.283 --> 00:04:08.411
My apologies to anybody here
of Japanese heritage.

00:04:08.453 --> 00:04:11.748
Today, it's an appropriate term
for much of the global economy.

00:04:11.748 --> 00:04:14.542
From Britain to Italy,
South Africa to Brazil,

00:04:14.542 --> 00:04:17.170
as one lost decade becomes two.

00:04:18.046 --> 00:04:19.506
And it gets worse.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.052
This economic malaise
we've seen over the last 15 years

00:04:24.093 --> 00:04:26.638
is surely to be joined
by the climate crisis,

00:04:26.638 --> 00:04:30.016
and then, demographic aging,
a crisis of elderly care.

00:04:30.016 --> 00:04:33.812
It turns out that the opening decades
of the 21st century,

00:04:33.812 --> 00:04:35.647
as bad as they were, for many,

00:04:35.688 --> 00:04:38.149
are merely the leading edge
of a hurricane.

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:40.360
Now ...

00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:44.030
despite everything I've just said to you,

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.323
I'm an optimist.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:52.372
I believe that humans have the ingenuity
to address all of these challenges

00:04:52.372 --> 00:04:55.959
and reach unprecedented prosperity
and liberty for all.

00:04:57.418 --> 00:05:00.255
That can happen by employing the state

00:05:00.255 --> 00:05:02.590
and leveraging the technology revolution.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:03.967
I wrote a book about it.

00:05:03.967 --> 00:05:07.220
It's called "Fully Automated
Luxury Communism."

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:08.221
(Laughter)

00:05:08.263 --> 00:05:09.430
The c-word.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:10.598
(Laughter)

00:05:11.933 --> 00:05:14.352
Fully automated, because we need
an economic system

00:05:14.352 --> 00:05:17.730
which reduces the necessity of human labor
in the production process.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.774
Luxury, because we need to expand

00:05:19.816 --> 00:05:22.694
the sense of liberty
and leisure time for all.

00:05:23.194 --> 00:05:27.615
Communism, because what I believe
is heading our way this century, maybe,

00:05:27.657 --> 00:05:29.951
could see the end
of production for exchange

00:05:29.951 --> 00:05:33.371
and the necessity of humans
to sell their labor for a wage.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:35.957
But in politics,

00:05:35.999 --> 00:05:37.584
big ideas only get you so far.

00:05:37.625 --> 00:05:40.044
That's been a problem
for the Left historically,

00:05:40.044 --> 00:05:41.337
I don't know if you know.

00:05:41.379 --> 00:05:44.257
And what matters
in the here and now, in 2022,

00:05:44.299 --> 00:05:46.092
are concrete proposals.

00:05:46.134 --> 00:05:49.178
So how do we leverage
the technology revolution?

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:50.972
How do we employ the state

00:05:50.972 --> 00:05:53.474
to address all the challenges
I've just spoken about,

00:05:53.516 --> 00:05:57.020
which I'm pretty sure everybody
in this room would acknowledge?

00:05:57.061 --> 00:06:00.648
Rising inequality,
the climate crisis, demographic aging.

00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:04.569
For some, the answer
is a universal basic income,

00:06:04.611 --> 00:06:06.404
a UBI.

00:06:07.113 --> 00:06:09.532
Now, despite being a millennial,

00:06:09.574 --> 00:06:12.619
and still petrified
when I look at my bank balance,

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:13.786
I'm not a fan.

00:06:14.287 --> 00:06:17.999
And the reason is an affordable
UBI is ineffective,

00:06:17.999 --> 00:06:20.960
and an effective UBI is unaffordable.

00:06:21.794 --> 00:06:25.298
My proposal, instead,
is universal basic services,

00:06:25.298 --> 00:06:26.466
UBS.

00:06:26.925 --> 00:06:29.344
These are services
which are universally available,

00:06:29.385 --> 00:06:30.970
free at the point of consumption

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:34.223
and paid for through progressive taxation,

00:06:34.223 --> 00:06:36.184
a bit like the NHS in the UK.

00:06:36.768 --> 00:06:40.188
I propose four of these universal
basic services --

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:43.900
health care, housing,
transport and education.

00:06:43.942 --> 00:06:45.109
Why these four?

00:06:46.527 --> 00:06:49.656
Housing, because you can't focus
on long-term problem-solving,

00:06:49.697 --> 00:06:51.282
or making something of yourself,

00:06:51.324 --> 00:06:54.118
if you have to move every 12 months.
Believe me, I know.

00:06:55.203 --> 00:06:57.747
Health care, because the basis
of everything else

00:06:57.747 --> 00:06:59.916
is physical and mental well-being.

00:06:59.958 --> 00:07:03.044
Education, because you can't be
of service to your community

00:07:03.086 --> 00:07:04.504
if you don't have skills,

00:07:04.545 --> 00:07:08.925
and we need to start acknowledging
that an educated society is a public good.

00:07:10.635 --> 00:07:15.306
People training as dentists,
as midwives, as engineers --

00:07:15.306 --> 00:07:16.933
hey, as accountants.

00:07:17.642 --> 00:07:18.893
We need those people.

00:07:18.935 --> 00:07:21.145
Society needs those people,
with those skills,

00:07:21.187 --> 00:07:23.523
to not just thrive, but to survive,

00:07:23.564 --> 00:07:26.234
and we all benefit
from them having those skills.

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:28.736
Transport, because you can
have the skills,

00:07:28.778 --> 00:07:31.364
you can have the housing,

00:07:31.406 --> 00:07:35.326
but location can remain a constraint
on access to opportunity.

00:07:35.368 --> 00:07:37.912
So, you might agree with me so far,

00:07:37.912 --> 00:07:39.622
you might say, "Aaron, I get it.

00:07:39.664 --> 00:07:41.749
Market failure exists, it's a thing.

00:07:41.749 --> 00:07:45.128
And yeah, OK, the state should intervene
in some areas, fine.

00:07:45.128 --> 00:07:46.379
But why universal?

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:51.300
Surely, we should focus scarce resources
on those that need help the most."

00:07:51.759 --> 00:07:54.887
It turns out that two academics
gave a pretty good answer to that,

00:07:54.929 --> 00:07:56.180
20 years ago.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.598
Surprise, they were Swedish.

00:07:57.598 --> 00:08:01.185
And they found that countries
with universal welfare systems

00:08:01.185 --> 00:08:05.189
saw the lowest rates of hardship,
the lowest rates of inequality,

00:08:05.231 --> 00:08:08.818
and universal welfare systems
commanded the broadest possible consent.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:13.990
If you want to see the citation,
it's Walter Korpi and Joachim Palme.

00:08:14.032 --> 00:08:16.034
Go on Google Scholar, find it.

00:08:16.617 --> 00:08:19.495
Now, how does that work?
Why is that the case?

00:08:19.495 --> 00:08:24.500
Well, it turns out
that universal welfare systems,

00:08:24.542 --> 00:08:28.296
because of the nature of how they work,

00:08:28.337 --> 00:08:30.465
have less bureaucracy.

00:08:30.506 --> 00:08:33.843
Because they don't employ means testing,
there's less stigma attached,

00:08:33.885 --> 00:08:37.138
so the people accessing resources
actually use them,

00:08:37.138 --> 00:08:39.265
as opposed to what we get
with means testing,

00:08:39.307 --> 00:08:41.559
where you feel like you shouldn't
be doing that.

00:08:41.601 --> 00:08:44.520
And importantly,

00:08:44.562 --> 00:08:47.899
they have the buy-in
of that political class

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.692
that is all-important in democracy,

00:08:49.734 --> 00:08:51.069
the middle class.

00:08:51.611 --> 00:08:55.323
There's a reason why the NHS in Britain

00:08:55.323 --> 00:08:58.618
is still around after 80 years,
and it's so loved.

00:08:58.659 --> 00:09:00.578
It's because everybody gets to use it.

00:09:00.578 --> 00:09:02.330
It's part of the national fabric.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:05.249
It’s part of our shared
social conversation and space.

00:09:05.291 --> 00:09:07.710
It’s something that belongs
to all of us in the UK,

00:09:07.752 --> 00:09:09.087
and we're very proud of it.

00:09:09.128 --> 00:09:13.508
So if you want welfare services
which address hardship,

00:09:13.549 --> 00:09:15.009
reduce inequality,

00:09:15.009 --> 00:09:17.929
are effective, efficient
and broadly liked,

00:09:17.929 --> 00:09:19.555
make them universal.

00:09:20.181 --> 00:09:25.061
"OK, I like universal services,
but how do we pay for this?"

00:09:25.103 --> 00:09:26.938
In a word, tax.

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.191
Hardly reinventing the wheel, I know.

00:09:31.275 --> 00:09:34.821
In the United States, in the 1950s,
the top rate of tax was 90 percent.

00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:36.989
Today, it's 37 percent.

00:09:37.698 --> 00:09:40.493
In the UK, for much
of Margaret Thatcher's premiership,

00:09:40.535 --> 00:09:42.870
the top rate of tax was 60 percent.

00:09:42.912 --> 00:09:44.622
Today, it's 45 percent.

00:09:44.664 --> 00:09:49.168
Dwight Eisenhower and the Iron Lady --
hardly two radical Marxists.

00:09:50.086 --> 00:09:53.256
And then, there's things
like financial transactions tax.

00:09:53.256 --> 00:09:55.716
All the outrage, in fact,

00:09:55.758 --> 00:10:00.263
that we tax work more than we tax wealth,

00:10:00.263 --> 00:10:01.681
which is astonishing.

00:10:01.722 --> 00:10:02.890
In the UK and the US,

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:05.268
capital gains are taxed
lower than incomes.

00:10:05.309 --> 00:10:07.937
How the hell does that work?
Talk about a rigged system,

00:10:10.231 --> 00:10:14.318
Then, there's the issue
around the technology revolution.

00:10:14.318 --> 00:10:19.657
And this is a big reason
why I like universal basic services.

00:10:19.657 --> 00:10:21.325
If I'm right,

00:10:21.325 --> 00:10:24.203
then the trends in the 21st century
are deflationary.

00:10:24.245 --> 00:10:29.292
Energy, information, labor
are getting cheap,

00:10:29.292 --> 00:10:30.501
they're deflationary,

00:10:30.501 --> 00:10:33.045
and they'll remain deflationary
for a very long time.

00:10:33.087 --> 00:10:36.382
Hard to believe, I know, in 2022,
when you're filling up at the tank,

00:10:36.424 --> 00:10:38.843
but renewables trends are clear,

00:10:38.843 --> 00:10:41.762
and that will be the case
for a very long time.

00:10:41.762 --> 00:10:46.893
Now, do we want those trends
to underpin universal basic services,

00:10:46.934 --> 00:10:48.603
or prop up shareholders,

00:10:48.603 --> 00:10:51.314
or create the basis
for new monopolistic models?

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.026
Finally, on the climate crisis,

00:10:55.067 --> 00:10:58.196
another reason
for universal basic services.

00:10:58.946 --> 00:11:02.241
A UBI would be an extraordinary amount
of money to spend.

00:11:02.241 --> 00:11:04.285
And yet, I don't see
quite what it would do

00:11:04.327 --> 00:11:07.872
in terms of transitioning our economies
away from fossil fuels,

00:11:07.914 --> 00:11:09.123
which again,

00:11:09.165 --> 00:11:11.292
I'm sure everybody
in this room acknowledges,

00:11:11.334 --> 00:11:12.960
we have to do pretty damn quickly.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:15.922
Meanwhile, with universal basic services,

00:11:15.963 --> 00:11:17.632
we can put a post-carbon agenda

00:11:17.673 --> 00:11:22.511
at the heart of education, health care,
transport and housing,

00:11:22.553 --> 00:11:24.639
rapidly decarbonizing our economies.

00:11:24.639 --> 00:11:26.891
And look, to move away from fossil fuels,

00:11:26.891 --> 00:11:30.269
we need to do the one thing
that market fundamentalists hate,

00:11:30.311 --> 00:11:31.771
and that's called planning.

00:11:32.939 --> 00:11:35.983
So, cast your minds to 2100,

00:11:35.983 --> 00:11:39.153
and a world after capitalism
as we know it.

00:11:39.695 --> 00:11:43.366
You go to your job four hours a day
in an elderly care center,

00:11:43.366 --> 00:11:47.203
one of the few labor-intensive industries
that's still around.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.832
Afterwards, you go for lunch,
you see your friends

00:11:51.874 --> 00:11:55.461
and you talk about taking that holiday
in some rewilded forest somewhere,

00:11:55.461 --> 00:11:58.422
go see some bison and some bears.

00:11:58.422 --> 00:12:01.550
And you talk about wanting to study
for that third college degree,

00:12:01.592 --> 00:12:02.843
this time in medicine,

00:12:02.843 --> 00:12:04.804
because you're working with older people

00:12:04.845 --> 00:12:08.599
and you're fascinated
by the sphere, the area,

00:12:08.641 --> 00:12:11.644
the growth industry
of radical life extension.

00:12:11.978 --> 00:12:14.814
And hey, your first two degrees
didn't cost a thing,

00:12:14.814 --> 00:12:16.983
and that second one,
East Asian Literature,

00:12:17.024 --> 00:12:19.235
gave you a whole new perspective on life.

00:12:19.235 --> 00:12:21.529
You really loved
Japanese poetry after that.

00:12:22.446 --> 00:12:23.948
Pretty good, right?

00:12:23.948 --> 00:12:26.909
You get a notification
as you leave that lunch.

00:12:27.326 --> 00:12:31.163
Your local health care clinic's saying
you need to go for a quick checkup.

00:12:31.664 --> 00:12:33.749
You go down there, you take a bus --

00:12:33.749 --> 00:12:36.127
free, electric, self-driving.

00:12:36.168 --> 00:12:38.921
The local bus co-op uses
a powerful predictive algorithm

00:12:38.963 --> 00:12:41.465
to determine how much supply
is needed at what time

00:12:41.465 --> 00:12:44.051
for optimal efficiency and effectiveness.

00:12:44.093 --> 00:12:46.887
Compare that to rush hour in LA or London.

00:12:47.221 --> 00:12:49.557
Big difference -- I know which I prefer.

00:12:49.598 --> 00:12:51.434
You end up at the local health clinic.

00:12:51.434 --> 00:12:53.728
They say, "Look, it's time
for liquid biopsy.

00:12:53.728 --> 00:12:55.438
You missed your checkup last week."

00:12:55.438 --> 00:12:57.773
You go in -- stage zero cancer.

00:12:57.773 --> 00:13:00.651
No problem, some pills will fix that.

00:13:01.861 --> 00:13:04.071
At every stage of this narrative,

00:13:04.113 --> 00:13:08.909
the health care, the transport,
the college degrees, the elderly care,

00:13:08.909 --> 00:13:11.620
we've seen universal
basic services in action.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:14.665
Universally available,
free at the point of consumption

00:13:14.707 --> 00:13:16.792
and paid for through progressive taxation.

00:13:17.251 --> 00:13:20.046
Now, this might sound utopian.

00:13:20.046 --> 00:13:22.840
Personally, I think
it's technically easier to do

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:24.925
than colonizing Mars,

00:13:24.967 --> 00:13:27.511
but our society
somehow thinks differently.

00:13:28.554 --> 00:13:29.764
But it's not utopian.

00:13:29.805 --> 00:13:33.184
In fact, in many ways,
this world resembles our own.

00:13:33.184 --> 00:13:35.978
There are still markets
for many, many things.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.397
The state isn't involved
in making chocolate bars

00:13:38.397 --> 00:13:40.483
or socks or silk ties.

00:13:40.483 --> 00:13:42.276
But it is the central player

00:13:42.276 --> 00:13:44.528
in these four things
we all need for liberty:

00:13:45.613 --> 00:13:48.991
housing, education,
health care and transport.

00:13:49.533 --> 00:13:54.246
And to say it's no utopia
means bad things still happen, yes.

00:13:54.288 --> 00:13:57.458
There are fallings out,
there's personal enmity,

00:13:57.500 --> 00:13:59.960
there are love affairs,
you fall in and out of love.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:02.838
Often, falling in love is worse,
it's more dangerous.

00:14:02.838 --> 00:14:04.173
These things still happen,

00:14:04.215 --> 00:14:06.550
but they're better than homelessness,

00:14:06.592 --> 00:14:08.677
than being unable to pay for your insulin,

00:14:08.719 --> 00:14:11.555
or failing to address
the climate crisis as a species.

00:14:13.641 --> 00:14:19.438
It’s time we lived up
to those glorious words:

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:23.526
life, liberty and the pursuit
of happiness.

00:14:23.567 --> 00:14:27.071
And recognize that, for the majority
of the global population,

00:14:27.071 --> 00:14:30.116
the pursuit of happiness is impossible.

00:14:30.157 --> 00:14:32.493
It's downright illusory,

00:14:32.535 --> 00:14:36.163
unless they have access
to universal basic services.

00:14:36.205 --> 00:14:39.792
This is the way by which
we guarantee liberty for all

00:14:39.834 --> 00:14:43.170
and address the great challenges
of the 21st century --

00:14:43.212 --> 00:14:46.340
the climate crisis,
inequality, demographic aging --

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.468
whilst leveraging
these remarkable technologies

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:52.221
that the ingenuity
of our species has created.

00:14:53.139 --> 00:14:56.976
I don't think there's another way
of addressing those challenges.

00:14:56.976 --> 00:15:01.772
I think anybody who thinks otherwise
is delusional, frankly.

00:15:03.399 --> 00:15:05.943
But all that means
we're going to have to do something

00:15:05.985 --> 00:15:08.988
which, for the political establishment
and status-quo thinking,

00:15:09.029 --> 00:15:10.489
has been anathema for decades.

00:15:10.489 --> 00:15:12.283
And that means returning the state --

00:15:12.324 --> 00:15:13.784
yes, the state --

00:15:13.826 --> 00:15:17.371
to the center stage
of our economic and social lives.

00:15:17.371 --> 00:15:18.539
Thank you.

00:15:18.581 --> 00:15:25.212
(Applause)

00:15:25.254 --> 00:15:27.423
Chris Anderson: Thank you, Aaron.

00:15:27.465 --> 00:15:30.342
I think we have
a secret questioner somewhere,

00:15:30.342 --> 00:15:36.390
in the form of Maja Bosnic,
who is a public finance expert.

00:15:36.432 --> 00:15:38.684
She spoke recently at TEDWomen.

00:15:38.726 --> 00:15:40.561
Maja, what's your question?

00:15:40.561 --> 00:15:42.480
Maja Bosnic: Yes, thank you very much.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:46.233
So my question is basically
related to resistances.

00:15:46.233 --> 00:15:48.402
I work with public services,

00:15:48.444 --> 00:15:51.989
and I work towards making them
more gender-responsive,

00:15:52.031 --> 00:15:55.576
so I know a thing or two
about resistances.

00:15:55.618 --> 00:15:59.955
So I wanted to ask you, what do you see
as the biggest resistances?

00:15:59.997 --> 00:16:03.792
And in which one,
or maybe some of these four

00:16:03.834 --> 00:16:05.252
that you are mentioning?

00:16:05.294 --> 00:16:06.462
Thank you.

00:16:06.462 --> 00:16:09.965
Aaron Bastani: Great question, thank you.
For me, it’s got to be housing.

00:16:09.965 --> 00:16:12.510
Because, particularly
in the Anglo-American economies,

00:16:12.551 --> 00:16:15.179
we have a growth model
built upon speculative investment

00:16:15.221 --> 00:16:16.388
in housing assets.

00:16:16.388 --> 00:16:18.891
The reality is, for the likes
of the UK and the US --

00:16:18.933 --> 00:16:22.520
the Canadian property market
is probably quite similar --

00:16:22.561 --> 00:16:25.064
we need to have something
akin to what Japan has seen

00:16:25.064 --> 00:16:26.232
over the last 25 years,

00:16:26.273 --> 00:16:28.817
which is, effectively,
flat house price increases.

00:16:28.859 --> 00:16:32.905
Sorry, I should phrase that better --
zero percent growth in house prices.

00:16:32.947 --> 00:16:34.657
So wages can catch up.

00:16:34.657 --> 00:16:37.618
I saw an amazing statistic, actually,
the other day in the UK,

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:40.412
which showed that a majority
of homeowners in the UK

00:16:40.454 --> 00:16:43.332
are happy to have zero growth
on house prices.

00:16:43.874 --> 00:16:45.000
And that makes sense.

00:16:45.042 --> 00:16:47.503
Why would you want
your house to gain in value?

00:16:47.503 --> 00:16:49.338
All it means is you buy another house,

00:16:49.338 --> 00:16:52.591
which has relatively gone up as well.

00:16:52.633 --> 00:16:55.177
And I think people acknowledge
that this isn’t working

00:16:55.219 --> 00:16:57.471
for people who rent,
who don't own the assets.

00:16:57.471 --> 00:17:00.140
And I would rather keep that price flat,

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:03.227
and, you know, have more people
included in our economy.

00:17:03.227 --> 00:17:06.355
But that's, I think,
the big structural challenge --

00:17:06.397 --> 00:17:09.275
we would have to take on
speculative investment in housing.

00:17:09.775 --> 00:17:11.235
CA: Thank you, Maja.

00:17:11.277 --> 00:17:17.366
Aaron, I'm really taken
by this argument for UBS,

00:17:17.366 --> 00:17:20.202
partly because, just structurally,

00:17:20.244 --> 00:17:22.746
some of those things
naturally get organized better

00:17:22.788 --> 00:17:25.499
if there's only one of them,
not a bunch in competition.

00:17:25.499 --> 00:17:29.920
You compare US health care capitalism
with something like the NHS

00:17:29.962 --> 00:17:32.339
or the state things,
which, for all their faults,

00:17:32.381 --> 00:17:35.301
seem to deliver equally good
or better health care

00:17:35.301 --> 00:17:37.011
for, like, half the money.

00:17:37.011 --> 00:17:40.180
Transport, you can make the same argument.

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:41.974
But have you cost it out?

00:17:41.974 --> 00:17:46.270
To roll out all these four,
that's a massive investment.

00:17:46.312 --> 00:17:48.188
Have you costed it?

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:49.732
AB: I haven't costed it.

00:17:49.773 --> 00:17:53.944
University College London
has costed universal basic services,

00:17:53.986 --> 00:17:57.531
and they had six -- they included food,
which is pretty ambitious.

00:17:58.365 --> 00:18:02.578
In the US private health care system,
16 percent of GDP's spent on health care.

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:03.954
In the UK, it's 10 percent.

00:18:03.996 --> 00:18:07.041
And we have longer life expectancy,

00:18:07.041 --> 00:18:08.667
fewer women dying in childbirth,

00:18:08.709 --> 00:18:10.210
lower infant mortality.

00:18:10.252 --> 00:18:12.171
So clearly, something is going right.

00:18:12.212 --> 00:18:14.298
And you might say,
"The NHS is underfunded."

00:18:14.298 --> 00:18:17.760
Bump up by one or two percent.
And of course, we have universal coverage.

00:18:17.801 --> 00:18:20.596
So there's a strong argument there
for public health care,

00:18:20.596 --> 00:18:22.640
from an efficiency perspective --

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:23.682
(Applause)

00:18:23.724 --> 00:18:24.892
AB: Thank you.

00:18:24.892 --> 00:18:26.393
(Applause)

00:18:26.435 --> 00:18:29.188
And I would respond with this,
in regards to elderly care.

00:18:29.229 --> 00:18:31.440
If you look at the crisis
of demographic aging,

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:33.901
and elderly care
that’s coming down the line

00:18:33.942 --> 00:18:37.446
because of lower birth replacement rates
of an aging population.

00:18:37.488 --> 00:18:42.201
And I want one of these geniuses
to come up with us living to 200.

00:18:42.201 --> 00:18:45.788
But if that happens,
we then create a crisis of elderly care,

00:18:45.788 --> 00:18:49.291
and the reality is, it's not
that we can't afford to do UBS,

00:18:49.333 --> 00:18:51.335
a UBS of elderly care, if that happens --

00:18:51.335 --> 00:18:52.795
we can't afford not to do it.

00:18:52.795 --> 00:18:56.507
Because I then return
to the NHS point about efficiency,

00:18:56.548 --> 00:18:57.966
particularly in elderly care.

00:18:57.966 --> 00:19:01.178
If you don't have that as a UBS,
you're in big trouble, in my view.

00:19:01.178 --> 00:19:04.682
CA: Aaron, thanks for an incredibly
compelling contribution to the debate.

00:19:04.723 --> 00:19:05.974
AB: Thank you, cheers.

00:19:05.974 --> 00:19:07.810
(Applause)

